08_admet_profiling.py — ADMET Profiling & Toxicity Risk Assessment
Log file: /home/andrey/Projects/__GITHUB__aavzpy2023/analysispaperKarimova2025/logs/08_admet_profiling.log
==========================================================================================

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
[EXPLAIN] What this step does
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  A molecule that binds the target is useless as a drug if it is toxic or cannot reach the
  bloodstream. This step         screens the docked candidates for three drug-likeness properties,
  using fast models trained on public data (TDC):
  1) Lipinski rule of five: a rule of thumb for oral drugs (limits on hydrogen-bond donors <= 5,
  acceptors <= 10, logP <= 5.0, plus molecular weight). Molecules that break it         tend to be
  poorly absorbed.         2) hERG: probability that the drug blocks the hERG potassium channel in
  the heart, a main cause of dangerous heart         rhythm side effects (QT prolongation).
  Probability < 0.5 is treated as low risk. Candidates above it are removed.         3) Caco-2:
  predicted permeability through a model of the intestinal wall (log cm/s, less negative = better
  absorbed).         Above -5.15 = moderate/high oral absorption; below it the drug is labelled
  'Parenteral Candidate' (better         given by injection) but is NOT removed.
  Limitations: both models are Random Forests on molecular fingerprints trained on a few hundred
  to a few thousand         compounds, so values are screening-level estimates, not experimental
  toxicity. Not modelled: liver metabolism,         blood-brain barrier (important for Toxoplasma,
  which infects the brain), and other off-target toxicity.
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
